SUMMARY:John Guy | Piprahwa and Relic Practice in Early Buddhist India - 
 An Archaeological Perspective
DESCRIPTION:The excavation of the Piprahwa relics in January\, 1898 - the
  most important Buddhist relic deposit ever recovered in India - immedia
 tely sparked enormous interest across the Buddhist world. Piprahwa stupa
  is located in northern Uttar Pradesh close to Lumbini\, the site of the
  historical Buddha's birthplace in western Nepal. The Piprahwa relic dep
 osit included the first reliquary inscription to credibly claim that it 
 contained the corporeal remains of Sakyamuni Buddha. The speaker will ex
 amine this unique cache of sacred objects in the context of early Buddhi
 st relic worship and emerging pan-Ind\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/events/
 2026/john-guy-piprahwa-and-relic-practice-early-buddhist-india-archaeolo
 gical-perspective
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/john-guy-piprahwa-and-relic-pra
 ctice-early-buddhist-india-archaeological-perspective
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
